    <node id="publication:32815018">
      <data key="d1">publication</data>
      <data key="d2">PMID 32815018</data>
      <data key="d3">32815018</data>
      <data key="d4">Clozapine-induced agranulocytosis.</data>
      <data key="d5">2020 Nov</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:32815018:1:00002">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">supported evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">32815018</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
      <data key="d7">Wider use of clozapine, one of the most effective antipshychotic drugs, is precluded by its propensity to cause agranulocytosis.</data>
      <data key="d8">supported</data>
      <data key="d9">[{"type": "causal_wording", "text": "cause", "start": 106, "end": 111}]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">3.949862763670301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:32815018:4:00003">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">supported evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">32815018</data>
      <data key="d6">4</data>
      <data key="d7">In this paper, we review the epidemiology of clozapine-induced agranulocytosis (CLIA), advances in identifying genetic risk factors, and the preventive measures to reduce the risk of CLIA.</data>
      <data key="d8">supported</data>
      <data key="d9">[{"type": "causal_wording", "text": "induced", "start": 55, "end": 62}]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">3.949862763670301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:32815018:6:00004">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">32815018</data>
      <data key="d6">6</data>
      <data key="d7">Finally, we address the clinical management and suggest the approach to clozapine re-challenge in patients with a previous episode of neutropenia.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[{"kind": "speculation", "text": "suggest", "start": 48, "end": 55}]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:32815018:7:00005">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">32815018</data>
      <data key="d6">7</data>
      <data key="d7">With a significant proportion of clozapine recipients in Western hemisphere being Black, we comment on the importance of recognizing benign ethnic neutropenia as a potential impediment to clozapine administration.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[{"kind": "speculation", "text": "potential", "start": 164, "end": 173}]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:32815018:8:00006">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">32815018</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">This review aims to aid haematologists and psychiatrists to jointly manage neutropenia and agranulocytosis caused by clozapine.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
