    <node id="publication:42624624">
      <data key="d1">publication</data>
      <data key="d2">PMID 42624624</data>
      <data key="d3">42624624</data>
      <data key="d4">Zuclopenthixol and clozapine-induced neutropenia.</data>
      <data key="d5">2026 Aug 20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42624624:1:00008">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">supported evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42624624</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
      <data key="d7">We highlight a case of zuclopenthixol-induced neutropenia, a rarely reported adverse effect (AE), and subsequent susceptibility to clozapine-induced neutropenia.</data>
      <data key="d8">supported</data>
      <data key="d9">[{"type": "causal_wording", "text": "induced", "start": 38, "end": 45}, {"type": "adverse_effect_wording", "text": "adverse effect", "start": 77, "end": 91}]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42624624:2:00009">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">supported evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42624624</data>
      <data key="d6">2</data>
      <data key="d7">Neutropenia is an uncommon but clinically significant haematological AE associated with several psychotropic agents, most notably clozapine.We describe a man in his 30s with treatment-resistant schizophrenia who developed medication-induced neutropenia during treatment with both zuclopenthixol and clozapine.</data>
      <data key="d8">supported</data>
      <data key="d9">[{"type": "associated", "text": "associated with", "start": 72, "end": 87}]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42624624:5:00010">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42624624</data>
      <data key="d6">5</data>
      <data key="d7">ANC normalised following drug discontinuation.Clozapine was subsequently initiated with a cautious titration; however, neutropenia reoccurred (1.31×10⁹/L), with a persistently reduced ANC for 15 days after cessation.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="publication:37910212">
      <data key="d1">publication</data>
      <data key="d2">PMID 37910212</data>
      <data key="d3">37910212</data>
      <data key="d4">[Clozapine-induced Tubulointerstitial Nephritis].</data>
      <data key="d5">2023 Aug 31</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:37910212:4:00011">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">37910212</data>
      <data key="d6">4</data>
      <data key="d7">Clozapine is a second generation atypical antipsycothic usually used for the treatment of schizophrenia resistant to other types of treatment; it can cause severe adverse effects among which the best known is a severe and potentially fatal neutropenia, furthermore a series of uncommon adverse events are recognized including hepatitis, pancreatitis, vasculitis.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[{"kind": "speculation", "text": "potentially", "start": 222, "end": 233}]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
