    <node id="publication:42212910">
      <data key="d1">publication</data>
      <data key="d2">PMID 42212910</data>
      <data key="d3">42212910</data>
      <data key="d4">Clozapine-Associated Neutropenia among People on Clozapine in Japan: A Nationally Representative Retrospective Cohort Study.</data>
      <data key="d5">2026 Apr 10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42212910:1:00267">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42212910</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
      <data key="d7">BACKGROUND AND HYPOTHESIS: Clozapine is the most effective antipsychotic for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, but use is constrained by potentially life-threatening neutropenia and mandatory hematological monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[{"kind": "speculation", "text": "potentially", "start": 138, "end": 149}]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42212910:9:00268">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42212910</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
      <data key="d7">Among clozapine-naïve patients, cumulative incidence was 1.8% for mild and 1.6% for serious neutropenia at 18 weeks, rising to 3.1% and 2.5% at 104 weeks, respectively.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42212910:10:00269">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">supported evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42212910</data>
      <data key="d6">10</data>
      <data key="d7">Faster clozapine titration rate was associated with higher incidence of serious neutropenia (sub-distribution hazard ratio [sHR] 1.22, 95% CI, 1.02-1.45 per 50 mg dosage increase at 2-weeks), as was older age (sHR 1.05, 95% CI, 1.04-1.06), while prior clozapine exposure was associated with lower incidence (sHR 0.27, 95% CI, 0.09-0.86).</data>
      <data key="d8">supported</data>
      <data key="d9">[{"type": "associated", "text": "associated with", "start": 36, "end": 51}]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:42212910:11:00270">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">42212910</data>
      <data key="d6">11</data>
      <data key="d7">CONCLUSIONS: In Japan, neutropenia among clozapine-treated patients accrued predominantly within the first 1-2 years, with faster titration in the first 2-weeks being associated with a 22% increase in serious neutropenia risk per 50 mg.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">4.379310388184409</data>
    </node>
