      <data key="d2">co_mention evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">27121434</data>
      <data key="d6">4</data>
      <data key="d7">In this report we review the existing data on late onset agranulocytosis with clozapine and describe the case of a young man, who developed agranulocytosis with clozapine after 35 months of treatment and was found to have genetic vulnerability in form of being positive for HLA DR4.</data>
      <data key="d8">co_mention</data>
      <data key="d9">[]</data>
      <data key="d10">[]</data>
      <data key="d11">case_study_lexicon_pair_scoped_rule_baseline</data>
      <data key="d12">3.949862763670301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evidence:27121434:5:00849">
      <data key="d1">evidence</data>
      <data key="d2">supported evidence</data>
      <data key="d3">27121434</data>
      <data key="d6">5</data>
      <data key="d7">This case highlights underlying autoimmune immune mechanism in clozapine-induced agranulocytosis and the need for frequent blood count monitoring on clozapine even after the initial 6 months of starting treatment especially in patients with genetic vulnerability to develop this condition.</data>
      <data key="d8">supported</data>
